{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1465930264,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUxNjAyNTYzNzU=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/546","origin":"github"},"title":"save() auto-adds untracked files -- is that what we want?","message":"So far I had conceptualized the addition of content to a dataset as the job of `install()` -- which it does fine. However, now `save()` can do it too: it runs `git annex add .` followed by `git add .` (based on my recommendation). The only use case that I can see where `install` is still required for adding content to a dataset is when file should be added to Git directly.\n\nNow the question: Really?\n\nThis implies that we cannot have untracked content by default with `save` (see --auto-add-changes=True). More general, it seems to communicate that we can have two ways of dealing with dataset changes:\n1. I don't give a fuck: `save -a`\n2. I am picky: `install \u003cpath\u003e`, `save --no-a`\n\nNow that I have written that it seems less disturbing than before, but we should still be on the same page prior setting it in stone @bpoldrack @yarikoptic","files":null}]}